/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:28:#    would redirect or merge them at the meeting point; the variant instead lets the gap with the smaller
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:31:#    The rule is simpler than the original collision algebra, so the generator must compensate by making
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:32:#    collisions common and readable. To keep the variant observable, generated grids use at least two
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:33:#    framed blocks, prefer 2-4 gaps per frame, and reject samples with zero collision stops. The ordering
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:34:#    key is visible: gaps are processed in row-major order of their locations, not by color ids or hidden
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:37:#    - _dir_diff / 45-degree / 90-degree branching assumed every collision would redirect; this is no
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:39:#    - truncate_beam/unpaint_cell/blocked assumed cells may need to be erased after a later collision.
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:40:#      That was harmless before because collisions could revise history; in the variant history is never
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:48:#    Frames remain separated by the original bbox gap and placed away from the border, so 4x4 frames do
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:53:#    Humans can see the ordering because gap positions themselves define it: upper gaps and then leftmost
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:54:#    gaps win ties. Training examples therefore show straight rays that terminate early at another straight
/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D2/0/code.py:55:#    ray instead of bending or thickening. Samples with no collision are rejected because they would look
